l Perspectives, 8, pp. 1-16.\n--- occurrences of iff / because / essence definitions ---\n1:# 03-grounding-essence-and-identity\n18:      related: grounding and essence. Yet how, exactly, the two are related remains\n19:      opaque. We develop a unified and uniform account of grounding and essence,\n20:      one which understands them both in terms of a generalized notion of identity\n23:      erning grounding, essence, and identity taken individually, and illuminates how\n25:      ternative unification of grounding and essence recently proposed by Kit Fine.\n31:grounding (when some phenomenon non-causally ‘derives’ from another) and es-\n35:what we call, following Linnebo (2014), generalized identity. Along with the intrin-\n40:What do we mean by “generalized” identity? Objectual identities (e.g. “Hesperus is\n41:Phosphorus”) are familiar, and display a canonical form: an identity-indicating\n47:  1 How grounding and essence interact is explicitly taken up in Audi (2012; 2015), Carnino\n48:(2014), Correia (2005; 2013), Dasgupta (2014; 2016), Fine (2012; 2015), Guigon (forthcoming),\n69:substitute in phrases like “is the same as” or “is no different than”, cancelling any\n84:identity involving it (Correia 2006, pp. 761-2; Dorr 2016, p. 40).3\n94:             be F is for it to be G iff the property of being F = the property of being G.\n97:             to be the case that q iff the fact that p = the fact that q.\n105:widely thought to govern objectual identity, to cover the “is” of statements like\n107:identity needn’t only be full, as in (1)-(4). It can also be partial, as when one says\n112:our account unifies grounding and essence by analyzing essence in terms of the\n113:conjunctive variety, and grounding in terms of the disjunctive variety.\n116:tity. In sections 2 and 3, we develop accounts of essence and grounding in terms\n117:of generalized identity, respectively. In section 4, we argue that the framework\n118:compares favorably to a unification of groundin