r ≤ q. Replacing ⊑∨ with ≤ in our\n1058:original account of grounding, and ⊑∧∨ with ≲, delivers the following subsump-\n1061:   (18)    p1, p2, … < q iff:   (i) p1 Ù p 2 Ù … ≤ q; and\n1065:grounding: Litland (2013, pp. 23-25) similarly takes p1, p2, … to ground q only\n1067:sense “[make] a difference to how [q] obtains”, and uses this condition to address\n1068:putative counterexamples to the transitivity of grounding due to Schaffer (2012).\n1070:for reductio that p < q, and yet q ≲ p (cases with several grounds can be treated\n1073:it—could never be: conjunctions don’t ground any of their conjuncts.\n1076:the literature that link grounding to notions in the vicinity of subsumption. For\n1077:instance, when Fine (2012, pp. 51-54) introduces the weak full grounds of a fact,\n1079:and that differ from the fact’s strict full grounds as they “may also move us side-\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
